{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain`, `NodeContents`, and `SourceNodes` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, evidence, and functions for running criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.public_api`. Every obligation in its text maps to a marked test, and the assertions enforce each one. I did not read the inventory or chapter declarations themselves. I relied on the test code's behavior and did not request any implementation source.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n- **One defining module path per item, and no public re-exports.**\n  - `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It asserts that no `use_declaration` has a plain `pub` visibility modifier.\n  - `fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` runs `cargo doc` for this source tree. It then walks the rustdoc item tables from the crate root (`fn[documented_exports]`).\n  - It builds the set of `(path, kind)` pairs and requires that set to equal the inventory in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`. Both missing and unexpected items fail the test, and a duplicate path fails it too.\n  - A re-export under a second path would appear as an item the inventory does not list, so this test also catches it.\n- **The listed public modules follow the chapters.** The same exports comparison covers the module tree. The inventory root must be a crate, and only modules may contain entries.\n- **Each item links to its owning requirement, which declares fields, variants, trait members and inherent methods.**\n  - `fn[documented_exports]` requires a declaration link for every non-module export.\n  - `fn[check_declaration]` reads the owning requirement's `rust,ignore` listing and checks that it has prose describing the item.\n  - It compares the requirement's trait implementations with rustdoc's.\n  - It compares the requirement's member names with rustdoc's indexed members.\n  - It compares the normalized declaration and inherent-method signatures from the requirement with the rustdoc output. This makes a change to the public interface require a change to the owning requirement.\n  - `fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` tests that comparison helper directly. It shows that reordering methods is accepted. It also shows that changing a field type, a return type or `&mut self` to `&self` is rejected, and that `(T,)` and `(T)` stay distinct.\n- **Public-field structs are `#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` asserts that a published struct with `structfield` members contains `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- **Constructors take the needed values, and builders consume `self` and take concrete values.**\n  - The signature comparison enforces these shapes against the requirement declarations. Because the listed signatures are compared textually, a generic or borrowed builder parameter would fail the test.\n  - Behavioral tests also exercise the constructors and builders.\n    - `agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` covers `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig`, `OllamaConfig` and `BedrockCredentials`.\n    - `outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` covers `Segment` and `SelectorError`.\n    - `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` covers `OutlineItem`, `Outline` and `OutlineAttachment`.\n    - `tests/source_defaults.rs` covers `SourceFile`, `TextQuery` and `StructuralQuery` in three tests: `fn[source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip]`, `fn[scoped_text_results_keep_line_numbers_context_and_completeness]` and `fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]`.\n- **Optional fields start as `None`.**\n  - `fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` asserts that `OutlineItem::name_span` is `None` and that `Outline::diagnostic` is `None`.\n  - The `source_defaults.rs` tests assert that `SourceFile::language` is `None` and that `TextQuery::limit` and `StructuralQuery::limit` are `None`.\n  - `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` shows the optional backend fields absent from the serialized form, such as `OpenAiConfig` serializing to `{}`.\n- **Backend inference options start empty and are added one key at a time.** `fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` shows no `options` key before any `.option(...)` call. It then adds `a` and `b` one at a time and replaces `a`'s value. The same test repeats the single-key addition on the OpenAI and Ollama configs.\n\n**Items I did not require tests for**\n\n- Constructors on `Fingerprint`, `Snapshot`, `OutlineId`, `Span`, `TextMatch`, `StructuralMatch` and `SearchResults` are only value pass-throughs. The signature comparison covers their shape. They have no conditionals or transformations and no stated \"stays unchanged\" obligation, so they fall under the testing exemption.\n- The closing sentence of the requirement, that source access, search engines, redaction and similar machinery are implementation details, is covered by the exports and inventory comparison. Any such item that became public would show up as an unexpected export.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gaps, and there were no `{fixme}` acknowledgments to account for.",
  "references": "83e5a024def69d2c249a2488b0d6e064785b414d91c24616a0f007d173d67065",
